  "result": {
    "session_id": "eval-20260427_230758-12-init-fail",
    "query_id": "12-init-fail",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "The deployment app-with-init in namespace scenario-test is stuck and never reaches Running state. The main application container never starts. Investigate why and identify the blocking component."
      }
    ],
    "final_text": "TARGETED: namespace=scenario-test, pod=app-with-init-7965877949-v9zlt, issue=Init container blocking main application startupThe issue is already clear from the snapshot:\n\n### Root Cause\nThe pod `app-with-init-7965877949-v9zlt` in the `scenario-test` namespace is stuck in the `Init` phase because its init container `db-check` is failing repeatedly. The init container is attempting to check database connectivity using the command:\n```sh\nnc -zw3 db.internal.svc.cluster.local 5432 || exit 1\n```\nThis fails because the host `db.internal.svc.cluster.local` does not exist or is unreachable, causing the init container to exit with code `1`. As a result, the main application container cannot start until the init container succeeds.\n\n### Blocking Component\nThe init container `db-check` is the blocking component. It is configured to fail if it cannot connect to the database service, which is either misconfigured or missing.\n\n### Recommended Fix\n1. Verify whether the database service `db.internal.svc.cluster.local` is correctly deployed and accessible.\n2. If the database service is not required for the application to start, modify the init container logic to skip the connectivity check or make it non-blocking.\n3. Alternatively, update the init container to use the correct database service hostname if it is misconfigured.\n\nWould you like me to:\n- Check if the database service exists in the cluster?\n- Patch the deployment to remove or modify the init container logic?",
